1
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:04,990
Hey, Bernie.

2
00:00:04,990 --> 00:00:07,790
Hey, how's my little astronautie hottie?

3
00:00:09,060 --> 00:00:10,270
Okay, I guess.

4
00:00:10,270 --> 00:00:12,280
It's just, being cooped up in this...

5
00:00:12,280 --> 00:00:14,880
tin can for weeks on end is starting to get to me.

6
00:00:14,900 --> 00:00:17,080
Well, hang in there. You just have a couple more days to go,

7
00:00:17,080 --> 00:00:19,190
- and then you'll be home. - I know.

8
00:00:19,530 --> 00:00:21,190
I got to get back to work.

9
00:00:21,190 --> 00:00:22,870
- I love you. - Love you too.

10
00:00:22,870 --> 00:00:24,040
Hey, Bernie?

11
00:00:24,040 --> 00:00:28,290
Before you go, can you do something for me?

12
00:00:28,290 --> 00:00:30,090
What do you want me to do?

13
00:00:30,090 --> 00:00:32,090
Okay, here it is.

14
00:00:32,540 --> 00:00:35,530
I really miss gravity.

15
00:00:35,530 --> 00:00:40,680
Can you drop something so I can watch it fall?

16
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:44,460
Really, you're serious?

17
00:00:48,310 --> 00:00:49,790
Okay.

18
00:00:51,820 --> 00:00:54,480
Oh, baby, you're killing me.

19
00:00:58,780 --> 00:01:00,020
Hey, Sheldon?

20
00:01:00,020 --> 00:01:02,150
This came for you today. It's from your mom.

21
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:04,910
Thank you, Penny.

22
00:01:04,920 --> 00:01:06,930
Yeah, wait... here.

23
00:01:09,640 --> 00:01:11,170
For your troubles.

24
00:01:12,280 --> 00:01:14,320
Oh, boy, a whole dollar.

25
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:16,760
Now, I can quit my paper route.

26
00:01:17,990 --> 00:01:19,700
- So, what's in it? - Mmm, doesn't matter.

27
00:01:19,700 --> 00:01:22,020
Half the time, he just ends up playing with the box.

28
00:01:23,180 --> 00:01:26,510
Yeah, it's journals and research papers I wrote as a child.

29
00:01:26,570 --> 00:01:28,940
Aw, how cute.

30
00:01:28,940 --> 00:01:30,270
Is this like a diary?

31
00:01:30,270 --> 00:01:33,100
No, that's my potty training journal.

32
00:01:33,890 --> 00:01:36,370
- Really, your potty training journal? - Yeah.

33
00:01:36,430 --> 00:01:38,200
And forgive my crude penmanship.

34
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:40,950
I didn't start typing until I was six.

35
00:01:43,090 --> 00:01:45,200
"August 7, 8:42 A.M.

36
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:48,010
This is humiliating. What was wrong with diapers?"

37
00:01:49,960 --> 00:01:51,990
There are some charts in the back

38
00:01:51,990 --> 00:01:54,830
where I kept track of shape, color and consistency.

39
00:01:56,780 --> 00:01:58,240
Disgusting.

40
00:01:58,240 --> 00:02:01,570
No, what's disgusting is he's still keeping track.

41
00:02:03,490 --> 00:02:05,110
- Why do you need all this stuff...? - No, no, no, no...

42
00:02:05,110 --> 00:02:07,500
- Sorry! - I am glad you asked.

43
00:02:07,980 --> 00:02:10,900
Yeah, are you familiar with the Higgs boson?

44
00:02:10,910 --> 00:02:14,430
Of course, it is... it's been in the news.

45
00:02:14,430 --> 00:02:17,050
And it's a very famous boson.

46
00:02:18,330 --> 00:02:19,360
Nice try.

47
00:02:19,360 --> 00:02:22,400
Now, in 1964, Dr. Peter Higgs,

48
00:02:22,400 --> 00:02:24,600
accomplished self-promoter and physicist,

49
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:27,130
he wrote a paper postulating the existence

50
00:02:27,130 --> 00:02:29,840
of a subatomic particle called the Higgs boson.

51
00:02:29,840 --> 00:02:32,620
Now, initially, the paper was rejected,

52
00:02:32,620 --> 00:02:34,990
but recently, he was proven right.

53
00:02:34,990 --> 00:02:38,140
And now he's on the fast track to win a Nobel prize.

54
00:02:38,140 --> 00:02:40,680
Yeah, that's basically what I said.

55
00:02:42,180 --> 00:02:43,670
The point is

56
00:02:43,670 --> 00:02:47,180
Higgs is being celebrated for work he did 50 years ago,

57
00:02:47,180 --> 00:02:48,410
so that got me thinking,

58
00:02:48,410 --> 00:02:50,780
perhaps I've already hit upon the idea

59
00:02:50,780 --> 00:02:53,060
that will win me my Nobel prize.

60
00:02:53,060 --> 00:02:54,840
I didn't know they gave Nobel prizes

61
00:02:54,840 --> 00:02:57,030
for making boom-boom in the potty.

62
00:02:59,310 --> 00:03:02,600
You really think there's some kind of scientific discovery in here?

63
00:03:02,600 --> 00:03:05,480
Well, this box only covers my work through nursery school.

64
00:03:05,480 --> 00:03:06,920
There's a good deal more to come.

65
00:03:06,920 --> 00:03:09,390
I didn't really hit my academic stride

66
00:03:09,390 --> 00:03:12,790
until I cut out that time-suck known as "playing outdoors."

67
00:03:13,880 --> 00:03:15,060
So, you're going to spend hours and hours

68
00:03:15,060 --> 00:03:16,400
combing through all this stuff?

69
00:03:17,120 --> 00:03:18,430
That's a good point.

70
00:03:18,430 --> 00:03:20,400
My time is much too valuable.

71
00:03:20,400 --> 00:03:22,890
You know, perhaps I should find someone to do it for me.

72
00:03:22,900 --> 00:03:25,390
You know, someone with a rudimentary understanding of science,

73
00:03:25,390 --> 00:03:29,100
but whose real talents lie in menial labor.

74
00:03:33,700 --> 00:03:35,360
Not gonna happen.

75
00:03:35,360 --> 00:03:37,850
Yeah, well, if I didn't think you could handle it,

76
00:03:37,850 --> 00:03:39,320
I wouldn't be asking.

77
00:03:40,020 --> 00:03:42,110
If you want help, just hire a grad student.

78
00:03:42,110 --> 00:03:43,270
Maybe I could do it.

79
00:03:43,270 --> 00:03:45,500
You, really?

80
00:03:46,260 --> 00:03:48,710
You can assess the quality of my work?

81
00:03:48,710 --> 00:03:50,310
Okay, um, here.

82
00:03:50,310 --> 00:03:52,540
I wrote this when I was five years old.

83
00:03:53,500 --> 00:03:56,300
"A proof that algebraic topology can never have

84
00:03:56,300 --> 00:04:00,060
a non-self contradictory set of abelian groups."

85
00:04:04,750 --> 00:04:06,960
I'm just a blonde monkey to you, aren't I?

86
00:04:10,950 --> 00:04:12,760
You said it, not me.

87
00:04:39,820 --> 00:04:42,740
All right, Ms. Jenson.

88
00:04:42,750 --> 00:04:45,520
Uh, before we begin, may I offer you a refreshment?

89
00:04:45,520 --> 00:04:47,760
Water, coffee, tea,

90
00:04:47,760 --> 00:04:49,670
a marijuana cigarette?

91
00:04:51,180 --> 00:04:52,340
No, thanks. I'm fine.

92
00:04:52,340 --> 00:04:53,690
Now, are you sure?

93
00:04:53,690 --> 00:04:55,800
Everyone's smoking them.

94
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:58,970
I think they're the best.

95
00:04:59,870 --> 00:05:00,920
I don't do drugs.

96
00:05:00,920 --> 00:05:02,250
Excellent, yeah.

97
00:05:02,250 --> 00:05:03,570
That was a ruse.

98
00:05:03,570 --> 00:05:05,050
They're not the best.

99
00:05:05,050 --> 00:05:07,020
Physics is the best.

100
00:05:07,580 --> 00:05:10,880
And by the way, coffee was also an unacceptable choice.

101
00:05:10,910 --> 00:05:14,590
All right, so I see here you're from Des Moines, Iowa.

102
00:05:14,590 --> 00:05:17,070
Uh, you're summa cum laude in theoretical physics

103
00:05:17,070 --> 00:05:18,750
from Stanford University.

104
00:05:18,750 --> 00:05:23,410
Oh, and two years ago, you had a persistent ear infection.

105
00:05:23,410 --> 00:05:25,710
I hope that didn't cause any hearing loss.

106
00:05:25,710 --> 00:05:27,190
No, of course not.

107
00:05:27,630 --> 00:05:29,370
How did you know about that?

108
00:05:29,370 --> 00:05:31,630
I did a comprehensive background check.

109
00:05:31,630 --> 00:05:35,390
Medical records, credit reports, criminal history.

110
00:05:35,390 --> 00:05:38,400
I trust you paid off those parking tickets.

111
00:05:38,890 --> 00:05:40,150
Yes, I did.

112
00:05:40,150 --> 00:05:42,380
Hearing unimpaired, good.

113
00:05:43,420 --> 00:05:45,860
Look, Dr. Cooper, I really want this position.

114
00:05:45,860 --> 00:05:47,970
It would be an incredible honor to work for

115
00:05:47,970 --> 00:05:49,550
a man of your brilliance.

116
00:05:49,760 --> 00:05:52,940
Flattery will not get you this job, Ms. Jenson.

117
00:05:53,480 --> 00:05:55,900
It's not flattery if it's the truth.

118
00:05:57,510 --> 00:05:59,280
Well, thank you. Welcome aboard.

119
00:06:01,380 --> 00:06:02,770
You talk to Howard lately?

120
00:06:02,770 --> 00:06:04,310
Uh, yeah, last night.

121
00:06:04,310 --> 00:06:07,240
He kept making me drop pencils for him.

122
00:06:09,020 --> 00:06:10,650
I got uncomfortable.

123
00:06:11,400 --> 00:06:13,300
Hey, Sheldon, hope you're hungry.

124
00:06:13,300 --> 00:06:14,760
They're serving macaroni and...

125
00:06:14,760 --> 00:06:17,060
cheese.

126
00:06:20,010 --> 00:06:21,450
Smooth.

127
00:06:24,230 --> 00:06:25,550
- Hi. - Hello.

128
00:06:25,850 --> 00:06:27,650
Sheldon, aren't you going to introduce us?

129
00:06:27,650 --> 00:06:29,720
No. I have people for that now.

130
00:06:29,720 --> 00:06:30,760
You're up.

131
00:06:31,460 --> 00:06:33,350
I'm Alex, Dr. Cooper's new assistant.

132
00:06:33,350 --> 00:06:36,400
Ah, congratulations and may God have mercy on your soul.

133
00:06:37,830 --> 00:06:39,160
I'm Leonard. This is Raj.

134
00:06:39,160 --> 00:06:40,470
It's nice to meet you.

135
00:06:40,470 --> 00:06:42,000
I'm so excited to be working with Dr...

136
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:44,900
Yeah, please reserve chitchat for your breaks, Ms. Jenson.

137
00:06:44,900 --> 00:06:47,650
Yeah, FYI, there will be no breaks.

138
00:06:49,240 --> 00:06:50,820
I should probably get to work.

139
00:06:54,050 --> 00:06:55,250
She seems nice.

140
00:06:55,250 --> 00:06:56,450
Hey, you already got a girlfriend.

141
00:06:56,450 --> 00:06:57,770
I call dibs.

142
00:06:59,020 --> 00:07:00,840
All I said was, "she seems nice."

143
00:07:00,840 --> 00:07:02,660
Yeah, well, I love her.

144
00:07:10,300 --> 00:07:12,810
Hey, I wasn't expecting to hear from you today.

145
00:07:12,810 --> 00:07:13,740
Everything okay?

146
00:07:13,740 --> 00:07:15,800
Uh, no, not really.

147
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:19,190
What's wrong?

148
00:07:19,190 --> 00:07:21,850
Well, remember the... Soyuz capsule

149
00:07:21,850 --> 00:07:23,320
they were sending to bring us home?

150
00:07:23,820 --> 00:07:25,290
It's delayed.

151
00:07:25,290 --> 00:07:26,940
We're gonna be here at least another week.

152
00:07:26,940 --> 00:07:27,750
Maybe ten days.

153
00:07:27,750 --> 00:07:30,580
It's the Russians, so you don't know.

154
00:07:33,410 --> 00:07:37,070
They left dogs up here in the '60s.

155
00:07:39,190 --> 00:07:40,130
Come on, Howard.

156
00:07:40,130 --> 00:07:41,900
No one's leaving you up there.

157
00:07:41,900 --> 00:07:43,940
I don't know how much longer I can take this.

158
00:07:43,940 --> 00:07:45,300
I can't sleep,

159
00:07:45,300 --> 00:07:48,660
and zero gravity is giving me crazy acid reflux.

160
00:07:49,220 --> 00:07:51,580
I'm down to my last three Tums.

161
00:07:53,690 --> 00:07:55,080
You're going to be fine.

162
00:07:55,080 --> 00:07:58,140
You survived that Weight Watchers cruise with your mom.

163
00:07:59,480 --> 00:08:02,460
And they ran out of low-fat ice cream on day two.

164
00:08:04,590 --> 00:08:07,300
Just calm down and take a few deep breaths.

165
00:08:07,300 --> 00:08:08,950
Okay, okay.

166
00:08:11,920 --> 00:08:14,210
What am I doing? I'm using up all the oxygen.

167
00:08:18,500 --> 00:08:21,440
If I die, promise you'll never have sex with another man.

168
00:08:28,430 --> 00:08:29,820
Oh, yeah.

169
00:08:32,460 --> 00:08:34,780
I'm a man-eater now.

170
00:08:36,480 --> 00:08:39,690
Okay, for the final touch, this is an eyelash curler.

171
00:08:39,690 --> 00:08:43,560
You just place it on your lashes and squeeze it closed.

172
00:08:43,560 --> 00:08:45,830
Oh, I don't know.

173
00:08:45,830 --> 00:08:48,790
Looks like something used by Tinker Bell's gynecologist.

174
00:08:52,180 --> 00:08:54,930
Who I hope for her sake is not Captain Hook, so...

175
00:08:59,940 --> 00:09:01,140
Who are you calling?

176
00:09:01,140 --> 00:09:02,540
I'm going to video-chat Sheldon.

177
00:09:03,580 --> 00:09:05,250
If my new look leads to phone sex,

178
00:09:05,250 --> 00:09:07,090
I'm going to have to ask you to leave the room.

179
00:09:09,050 --> 00:09:10,670
Dr. Cooper's office.

180
00:09:10,670 --> 00:09:12,720
Oh, hello. Who are you?

181
00:09:12,720 --> 00:09:14,920
I'm Alex, Dr. Cooper's assistant.

182
00:09:14,920 --> 00:09:15,920
Can I help you?

183
00:09:15,920 --> 00:09:17,390
I'd like to speak to Sheldon.

184
00:09:17,390 --> 00:09:19,920
I'm sorry. He's asked me to hold all calls

185
00:09:19,930 --> 00:09:21,620
unless you're Stephen Hawking, his mother

186
00:09:21,620 --> 00:09:23,860
or himself from the future.

187
00:09:25,680 --> 00:09:26,240
All right.

188
00:09:26,240 --> 00:09:28,420
- Well, tell him Amy called. - Last name?

189
00:09:28,420 --> 00:09:29,470
He knows my last name.

190
00:09:29,470 --> 00:09:31,400
I'm his girlfriend.

191
00:09:31,920 --> 00:09:33,060
We have a contract and everything.

192
00:09:33,060 --> 00:09:35,030
I'll send you a PDF.

193
00:09:37,200 --> 00:09:39,500
All right, I will tell him you called.

194
00:09:41,480 --> 00:09:43,650
- Hmm, that's weird.  - What?

195
00:09:44,300 --> 00:09:47,090
Sheldon told me he had a new assistant named Alex.

196
00:09:47,090 --> 00:09:49,250
He didn't mention that Alex was a girl.

197
00:09:49,250 --> 00:09:51,000
Maybe he didn't notice.

198
00:09:52,490 --> 00:09:53,200
You're right.

199
00:09:53,200 --> 00:09:54,600
I don't have to worry about Sheldon.

200
00:09:54,600 --> 00:09:56,760
No, you do not.

201
00:09:59,450 --> 00:10:01,030
It's her I have to worry about.

202
00:10:01,030 --> 00:10:02,720
Oh, Amy, really?

203
00:10:02,720 --> 00:10:05,500
- She is not going to come on to Sheldon.  - Oh, really?

204
00:10:06,230 --> 00:10:07,900
Look at this face.

205
00:10:11,290 --> 00:10:13,450
How can any woman spend eight hours a day

206
00:10:13,450 --> 00:10:15,950
alone with this face and not fall in love with it?

207
00:10:17,900 --> 00:10:19,100
Well, for starters,

208
00:10:19,100 --> 00:10:21,420
at some point, that face starts talking.

209
00:10:27,660 --> 00:10:28,890
Amy, this is crazy.

210
00:10:28,890 --> 00:10:30,590
You have nothing to be suspicious about.

211
00:10:30,590 --> 00:10:31,880
I'm not suspicious.

212
00:10:31,880 --> 00:10:34,250
I just want to stop in, say hi to my boyfriend

213
00:10:34,250 --> 00:10:35,670
and meet his new assistant.

214
00:10:42,120 --> 00:10:44,420
- Can we go now?  - Hang on.

215
00:10:51,600 --> 00:10:52,600
What are you doing?

216
00:10:52,600 --> 00:10:54,200
Isn't it obvious?

217
00:10:54,200 --> 00:10:57,360
I'm spreading my scent to mark my territory.

218
00:11:00,150 --> 00:11:02,840
- Come on, Amy. That is not gonna work.  - Really?

219
00:11:02,840 --> 00:11:05,140
Because just before you became my best friend,

220
00:11:05,140 --> 00:11:07,520
I did this all over your apartment.

221
00:11:12,750 --> 00:11:15,340
Has your, uh, assistant said anything about me?

222
00:11:15,340 --> 00:11:16,950
In fact, she has.

223
00:11:16,950 --> 00:11:20,580
Uh, her exact words were, "What is that guy's problem?"

224
00:11:22,510 --> 00:11:25,860
I'm in her head. Let the dance begin.

225
00:11:28,040 --> 00:11:30,360
Here's your frozen yogurt, Dr. Cooper.

226
00:11:30,360 --> 00:11:31,680
This should be fun.

227
00:11:31,680 --> 00:11:33,310
Half chocolate, half vanilla,

228
00:11:33,310 --> 00:11:35,070
- side by side, not swirled? - Yes.

229
00:11:35,070 --> 00:11:37,270
Half a teaspoon of sprinkles? Rainbow, not chocolate.

230
00:11:37,270 --> 00:11:39,010
- Two cherries?  - One on top, one on the bottom.

231
00:11:39,010 --> 00:11:40,330
- Stems removed?  - Um...

232
00:11:41,350 --> 00:11:42,280
top one, yes.

233
00:11:42,280 --> 00:11:43,940
I didn't check the one on the bottom.

234
00:11:47,470 --> 00:11:49,470
I'm so sorry, Dr. Cooper.

235
00:11:49,470 --> 00:11:50,460
It's all right, Alex.

236
00:11:50,460 --> 00:11:53,420
I'm not mad at you; I'm just disappointed.

237
00:11:55,360 --> 00:11:57,100
Wait, Alex. Do you want to join us?

238
00:11:58,100 --> 00:11:59,170
Uh, Alex, a moment.

239
00:11:59,170 --> 00:12:01,200
I need a word with Dr. Hofstadter.

240
00:12:04,710 --> 00:12:06,310
Do you think it's appropriate for her to sit with us,

241
00:12:06,310 --> 00:12:08,360
given her station?

242
00:12:09,950 --> 00:12:11,430
Given her what?

243
00:12:11,820 --> 00:12:15,140
If I've learned anything from British television shows on PBS,

244
00:12:15,140 --> 00:12:19,450
it's that servants dine downstairs with their own kind.

245
00:12:20,860 --> 00:12:22,150
What?

246
00:12:23,120 --> 00:12:25,000
It's a kindness, Leonard.

247
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,940
Otherwise, you're cruelly tempting them with a glimpse

248
00:12:27,940 --> 00:12:31,300
of a world that's just beyond their soot-stained fingertips.

249
00:12:32,730 --> 00:12:34,430
Oh, sure.

250
00:12:34,430 --> 00:12:35,700
Please join us.

251
00:12:35,700 --> 00:12:38,070
Oh, okay. Thank you.

252
00:12:38,070 --> 00:12:41,020
So, Alex, what's the topic of your dissertation?

253
00:12:41,020 --> 00:12:42,850
I'm looking for Trojan asteroids

254
00:12:42,850 --> 00:12:44,550
at Earth's L-Five Lagrange point.

255
00:12:44,550 --> 00:12:46,460
Oh, that happens to be

256
00:12:46,460 --> 00:12:48,560
Dr. Koothrappali's field of expertise.

257
00:12:48,560 --> 00:12:50,640
You two have a lot to talk about.

258
00:12:53,640 --> 00:12:55,010
Is that true?

259
00:13:06,470 --> 00:13:07,750
Is he all right?

260
00:13:07,750 --> 00:13:09,270
No.

261
00:13:13,080 --> 00:13:14,730
But compared to your boss,

262
00:13:14,730 --> 00:13:17,150
he's the poster boy for sanity.

263
00:13:17,800 --> 00:13:20,130
It's okay, I've been around scientists all my life.

264
00:13:20,130 --> 00:13:21,970
My dad's an astronomer at SETI.

265
00:13:21,970 --> 00:13:25,020
Oh, SETI-- the Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence.

266
00:13:25,020 --> 00:13:27,380
You should introduce him to Sheldon. The search is over.

267
00:13:29,330 --> 00:13:31,150
So, what kind of research are you doing?

268
00:13:31,150 --> 00:13:33,220
- High-energy lasers.  - Ooh. Military?

269
00:13:33,530 --> 00:13:37,050
Not yet, but I can remove unwanted hair from two miles away.

270
00:13:38,980 --> 00:13:39,700
You were right.

271
00:13:39,700 --> 00:13:41,180
I had nothing to worry about.

272
00:13:41,180 --> 00:13:43,480
That skank's your problem, not mine.

273
00:13:45,270 --> 00:13:48,060
- You're very funny, Dr. Hofstadter.  - Thank you.

274
00:13:48,040 --> 00:13:50,450
Aah! Cherry stem! Cherry stem!

275
00:13:54,270 --> 00:13:56,700
How you doing, Howie? You feeling a little better?

276
00:13:56,700 --> 00:14:01,380
Oh, a lot better, thanks. One sec.

277
00:14:02,880 --> 00:14:04,800
Listen close. I don't have a lot of time.

278
00:14:06,660 --> 00:14:07,670
I need you to go to my house.

279
00:14:07,670 --> 00:14:09,180
In my bedroom, you'll find a model rocket.

280
00:14:09,180 --> 00:14:11,200
I want you to take it and bring it back to your place.

281
00:14:12,810 --> 00:14:13,760
Okay...

282
00:14:13,760 --> 00:14:16,780
Step 2: build a version roughly 14 stories high.

283
00:14:18,230 --> 00:14:20,610
Fill it full of rocket fuel and come get me.

284
00:14:20,610 --> 00:14:22,630
I'll leave the door unlocked.

285
00:14:25,480 --> 00:14:27,310
Howie, honey, maybe you should talk to someone,

286
00:14:27,310 --> 00:14:29,390
let them know you're having a little anxiety.

287
00:14:29,390 --> 00:14:31,550
No, no, I'm fine.

288
00:14:31,550 --> 00:14:33,290
No anxiety!

289
00:14:33,820 --> 00:14:35,910
We should probably talk in code.

290
00:14:35,910 --> 00:14:38,710
From now on, "Frog" is me, "Sandwich" means you

291
00:14:38,710 --> 00:14:41,200
and "Lemon" means rocket. So, come on, sandwich,

292
00:14:41,200 --> 00:14:43,920
build me a lemon 'cause froggy wants to come home!

293
00:14:49,660 --> 00:14:54,020
Look at you, putting on a brave face.

294
00:14:54,810 --> 00:14:56,240
There's nothing to be brave about.

295
00:14:56,240 --> 00:14:58,680
- Everything's fine. - Really?

296
00:14:59,420 --> 00:15:01,100
I don't know how much you know about primate behavior,

297
00:15:01,100 --> 00:15:03,870
but Sheldon's assistant was clearly courting Leonard.

298
00:15:04,790 --> 00:15:06,460
Were she a mandrill, she would have bent over

299
00:15:06,460 --> 00:15:08,500
and displayed her brightly colored hindquarters

300
00:15:08,500 --> 00:15:10,240
like a big red welcome mat.

301
00:15:12,940 --> 00:15:14,810
By the way, you try that at the junior prom,

302
00:15:14,810 --> 00:15:16,260
you get kicked out.

303
00:15:18,330 --> 00:15:21,850
Okay, maybe she was flirting with him, but who cares?

304
00:15:21,850 --> 00:15:24,760
Look, I don't even know where my relationship is with Leonard is right now.

305
00:15:24,760 --> 00:15:26,850
So says your prefrontal cortex.

306
00:15:26,850 --> 00:15:28,970
But meanwhile, the limbic system of your brain is calculating

307
00:15:28,980 --> 00:15:31,740
that if another woman is attracted to Leonard,

308
00:15:31,740 --> 00:15:33,140
it must be because he's desirable.

309
00:15:33,150 --> 00:15:35,650
Well, of course he's desirable.

310
00:15:35,650 --> 00:15:37,420
I mean, he's great.

311
00:15:37,420 --> 00:15:39,070
He's smart, he's sweet,

312
00:15:39,070 --> 00:15:40,530
and, ooh, in the bedroom, whew,

313
00:15:40,530 --> 00:15:43,520
let me tell you, he really tries.

314
00:15:45,900 --> 00:15:48,060
So it doesbother you.

315
00:15:48,060 --> 00:15:50,700
Fine, it bothers me a little.

316
00:15:51,340 --> 00:15:52,430
No. You know what? This is stupid.

317
00:15:52,430 --> 00:15:53,830
It doesn't bother me.

318
00:15:55,470 --> 00:15:56,900
Okay, it bothers me.

319
00:15:58,450 --> 00:15:59,800
But only because she wouldn't stop laughing.

320
00:15:59,800 --> 00:16:01,270
Leonard is not that funny.

321
00:16:03,680 --> 00:16:04,980
And there you have it;

322
00:16:04,980 --> 00:16:07,810
prefrontal cortex reasoning versus limbic lust.

323
00:16:08,570 --> 00:16:10,430
If this were a boxing match, they might call it

324
00:16:10,430 --> 00:16:13,380
"The thrilla adjacent to the amygdala."

325
00:16:16,880 --> 00:16:18,260
If you were a brain scientist,

326
00:16:18,260 --> 00:16:20,870
you would be busting a gut right now!

327
00:16:24,780 --> 00:16:26,140
So, how was work today?

328
00:16:26,140 --> 00:16:27,810
Ah, it was all right, I guess.

329
00:16:27,810 --> 00:16:30,480
Got to Heimlich a cherry stem out of Sheldon.

330
00:16:31,900 --> 00:16:33,410
Caught Raj right in the eye.

331
00:16:35,630 --> 00:16:36,870
You're kidding.

332
00:16:36,870 --> 00:16:38,590
No, Raj had to go to the nurse.

333
00:16:38,590 --> 00:16:41,080
Wow. Anything else?

334
00:16:41,080 --> 00:16:44,540
Mmm, the nurse is a woman, so he couldn't talk to her.

335
00:16:45,760 --> 00:16:47,090
She had to bring him a Grover puppet

336
00:16:47,090 --> 00:16:49,010
so he could point at what hurt.

337
00:16:53,310 --> 00:16:54,990
Is that it?

338
00:16:54,990 --> 00:16:56,960
Isn't that enough?

339
00:16:56,960 --> 00:16:58,930
It had the weaponized fruit and a puppet.

340
00:16:58,930 --> 00:17:00,590
What more do you want?

341
00:17:01,710 --> 00:17:03,150
Oh, good, Leonard, you're here.

342
00:17:03,150 --> 00:17:04,440
Science news.

343
00:17:04,440 --> 00:17:05,820
This will interest you.

344
00:17:05,820 --> 00:17:08,820
And, Penny, feel free to paint your nails.

345
00:17:09,880 --> 00:17:10,920
What do you got?

346
00:17:10,920 --> 00:17:12,950
I believe Alex may have unearthed

347
00:17:12,950 --> 00:17:14,560
the grade school science project

348
00:17:14,560 --> 00:17:17,070
that could be my ticket to the Nobel Prize.

349
00:17:17,070 --> 00:17:18,360
Behold.

350
00:17:18,360 --> 00:17:21,230
"Magnet What Do They Stick To?"

351
00:17:23,120 --> 00:17:26,080
If the answer is "Metal," It's not exactly groundbreaking.

352
00:17:26,800 --> 00:17:29,260
The original title was "A Rederivation

353
00:17:29,260 --> 00:17:32,470
of Maxwell's Equations Regarding Electromagnetism."

354
00:17:32,470 --> 00:17:33,540
I dumbed it down

355
00:17:33,540 --> 00:17:35,560
because some of the more religious people in town

356
00:17:35,560 --> 00:17:37,440
were starting to say I was a witch.

357
00:17:39,100 --> 00:17:41,440
Sheldon thinks the approach in this paper might change the way

358
00:17:41,440 --> 00:17:43,890
we calculate ferromagnetic hysteresis.

359
00:17:43,890 --> 00:17:46,550
Oh, it's about time. I hated the old way.

360
00:17:47,850 --> 00:17:49,740
- Hi. I'm Penny. - Alex.

361
00:17:49,740 --> 00:17:51,610
Uh, do you work with Dr. Hofstadter?

362
00:17:51,610 --> 00:17:52,990
In a way.

363
00:17:55,600 --> 00:17:59,160
We've kind of been involved in a five-year experiment.

364
00:17:59,160 --> 00:18:01,900
Oh. Well, you're lucky. He seems very talented.

365
00:18:01,900 --> 00:18:04,130
And I'm sure a lot of people want to work with him.

366
00:18:04,130 --> 00:18:06,810
Well, a lot of people can't.

367
00:18:08,290 --> 00:18:09,130
Come on.

368
00:18:10,070 --> 00:18:11,490
Where are we going?

369
00:18:11,490 --> 00:18:14,060
My limbic system wants to take your pants off.

370
00:18:17,140 --> 00:18:18,610
She seems nice.

371
00:18:19,820 --> 00:18:22,170
Notice people on your own time. We're working.

372
00:18:28,810 --> 00:18:30,660
Hey, Howie, how you feeling?

373
00:18:30,660 --> 00:18:32,500
Better. Much better.

374
00:18:32,500 --> 00:18:35,690
The other astronauts held me down,

375
00:18:35,690 --> 00:18:37,360
gave me a shot.

376
00:18:46,640 --> 00:18:49,290
Attention, people of Earth:

377
00:18:49,290 --> 00:18:55,640
Tonight, there will be two moons in the sky.

378
00:18:56,460 --> 00:18:58,110
Howie, stop that.

379
00:18:58,450 --> 00:19:00,330
NASA's watching this!

380
00:19:00,330 --> 00:19:02,400
Put your pants back on!

